Type mapping (C ABI-identical):
  ff_error_t  <-> Int32            (C enum = int = 32-bit)   [note: cancel/pause/resume use ff_error_t to match header exactly]
  const char* <-> UnsafePointer<CChar>
  int         <-> Int32
  uint64_t    <-> UInt64
  uint64_t *  <-> UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt64>?
  size_t      <-> Int
  FFSearchCallback <-> @convention(c) (UnsafeRawPointer?, UnsafeMutableRawPointer?) -> Void
  void *      <-> UnsafeMutableRawPointer?
  char * (owned) <-> UnsafeMutablePointer<CChar>?

Note on cancel/pause/resume return type: the header declares `ff_error_t`, and the C function
is ALREADY imported into Swift via the bridging header (ff_ffi.h). Redeclaring these scalar-only
(UInt64) functions with `-> Int32` produces an ambiguous overload (same param, differing return
type). Declaring them `-> ff_error_t` matches the header exactly and merges cleanly.
